# Native input routing

Status: in progress; Phases 1-2 complete

Related implementation:

- `src/dwarfspec/native_attachment.lua`
- `src/dwarfspec/interaction_target.lua`
- `src/dwarfspec/ds.lua`
- `tests/unit/native_attachment_spec.lua`
- `tests/unit/interaction_target_spec.lua`
- `tests/unit/ds_mount_commands_spec.lua`
- `tests/automation/native_screen_mount_live_spec.lua`
- `tests/automation/native_input_routing_live_spec.lua`
- `tests/automation/support/native_input_fullscreen_probe.lua`

## Purpose

Make `ds.mountNativeScreen()` route simulated player input through the
viewscreen that is current at dispatch time while preserving the deliberately
targeted behavior of `ds.mount(component)`.

The native mount continues to borrow the base DF viewscreen and its widgets for
subject lookup. It does not own that screen, and the base DF screen does not
have to be the top screen while the mount is active.

This checklist supersedes conflicting native-screen transition requirements in
`docs/mount-command-automation-coverage.todo`.

## Acceptance contract

☐ Component mounts dispatch input only within their owned screen boundary.
☐ An unrelated current screen never becomes a component mount's input target.
☐ Native mounts can attach while a registered fullscreen DFHack overlay is
  current.
☐ Native subject lookup remains rooted at the borrowed base DF widgets.
☐ Immediately before every native `gui.simulateInput` call, DwarfSpec resolves
  and explicitly passes the current top viewscreen.
☐ DwarfSpec does not manually replay unhandled input; normal DFHack handling
  controls consumption and pass-through.
☐ Showing or dismissing a fullscreen screen changes subsequent native input
  routing without requiring remounting.
☐ A top-screen change alone does not make the native mount or its subjects
  stale.
☐ Genuine subject removal, replacement, disablement, or source invalidation
  remains an explicit stale-subject failure.
☐ Unmounting never dismisses, replaces, resizes, or navigates borrowed screens.
☐ Pointer, button, focus, screen-stack, overlay, and cleanup state is restored
  after successful and failed tests.

## Scope

Included:

- separating owned-component input targets from native input ingress;
- accepting a native mount beneath a registered fullscreen overlay;
- dynamically resolving the native input target;
- unit and live regression coverage;
- reconciling the older coverage checklist.

Excluded:

- changing the public mount APIs;
- targetless `gui.simulateInput`;
- custom input bubbling or direct `onInput()` calls;
- changing native or overlay subject-source selection;
- changing pointer coordinate-system behavior;
- redesigning redraw or focus-list behavior.

## Phase 1: Implement mount-specific input routing

### Tasks

✔ Record the initial repository identity and worktree state.
✔ Replace the shared `native_screen(...)` input concept with an explicit
  mount-specific input-target operation.
✔ Preserve the owned-component policy:
  - require the owned host to remain mounted and active;
  - target its native host;
  - allow a current descendant only when it belongs to the owned host's screen
    chain;
  - never select an unrelated current screen.
✔ Implement the borrowed-native policy:
  - query the current top viewscreen immediately before each
    `gui.simulateInput` call;
  - explicitly pass the resolved screen to `gui.simulateInput`;
  - report a bounded error when the current screen cannot be queried or is
    absent.
✔ Refactor native attachment to validate and borrow the base DF viewscreen and
  its widget root independently from the current top screen.
✔ Remove the requirement that the base DF viewscreen be current when attaching
  or dispatching native input.
✔ Remove top-screen identity changes as a native mount and subject staleness
  condition.
✔ Preserve widget identity and source-lifecycle staleness checks.
✔ Route all keyboard, text, click, button, and wheel commands through the
  mount-specific input-target operation.
✔ Resolve the native target once immediately before each actual
  `gui.simulateInput` call; if a command makes multiple calls, resolve each one
  independently.
✔ Preserve existing redraw, focus-list, pointer placement, and temporary
  button-state behavior except where they invoke the shared input dispatcher.
✔ Preserve partial-construction cleanup, the single-active-mount rule, and
  primary failure reporting.
✔ Update `docs/mount-command-automation-coverage.todo` to remove requirements
  that:
  - reject mounting beneath a fullscreen DFHack screen;
  - mark subjects stale solely because the top screen changed;
  - require the borrowed base DF screen to remain current.
✔ Add or update LuaDoc comments for every changed function, method, class, and
  class-like table.

### Phase 1 exit gate

✔ Owned and native mounts have distinct input-target policies.
✔ Every simulated input call receives an explicit non-nil target.
✔ Native attachment and input work when another screen is current.
✔ Non-input behavior and genuine staleness checks remain intact.
✔ The two tasklists describe the same routing contract.

## Phase 2: Add regression coverage

### Unit coverage

✔ Prove an owned target uses its host or an owned descendant.
✔ Prove an owned target rejects or ignores an unrelated current screen.
✔ Prove a borrowed native target queries the current top screen for every input
  dispatch and follows screen changes without remounting.
✔ Prove native attachment succeeds beneath a different current screen while
  retaining the base DF widget root.
✔ Prove representative keyboard, text, click, button, and wheel routes use the
  shared mount-specific dispatch boundary.
✔ Prove every simulated input receives an explicit target.
✔ Prove retained native subjects survive top-screen changes when their widget
  identities remain valid.
✔ Preserve tests for genuine widget removal, replacement, invalid roots,
  dependency failures, partial construction, and post-cleanup use.
✔ Prove component input remains targeted after the refactor.
✔ Prove cleanup mutates no base, fullscreen, or parent screen.

### Live automation

✔ Add a test-owned registered fullscreen overlay that records normal keyboard
  and mouse input through its public lifecycle.
✔ Give the overlay deterministic registration, enablement, rendering,
  consumption, dismissal, and independent cleanup.
✔ Do not call its `onInput()` method directly from the test.
✔ Show the overlay before `ds.mountNativeScreen()` and prove mounting succeeds.
✔ Prove native root lookup still reads the borrowed base DF widgets.
✔ Dispatch representative keyboard input and prove the current overlay
  receives it.
✔ Place the real pointer over a rendered target, dispatch representative mouse
  input, and prove the current overlay receives it.
✔ Prove consumed input is not replayed by DwarfSpec.
✔ Prove unconsumed input follows normal DFHack pass-through with an observable,
  reversible backing-screen result.
✔ Dismiss and reshow the overlay during the same native mount and prove
  subsequent input follows each current screen without remounting.
✔ Retain and use a native subject across those transitions.
✔ Prove `ds.unmount()` leaves the overlay and base DF screen untouched.
✔ Independently restore overlay registration and configuration, focus, screen
  stack, pointer/button state, reversible backing state, and DwarfSpec cleanup.

### Phase 2 exit gate

✔ Unit tests cover all input command families through the shared dispatch
  boundary without duplicating every command live.
✔ Live keyboard and mouse input enter through the registered fullscreen
  overlay.
✔ Handled and pass-through behavior are separately proven.
✔ Component targeting, retained native subjects, and exact cleanup are proven.

## Phase 3: Verify and close

### Tasks

☐ Run the focused native attachment, interaction target, and mount-command unit
  specifications.
☐ Run the focused native-screen automation specification and require
  `cleanup_confirmed=true`.
☐ Run the full unit suite.
☐ Run Lua syntax and formatting checks.
☐ Run the full automation suite and require confirmed cleanup.
☐ Build and inspect the local rock because packaged production source changed.
☐ Verify the rock contains changed production modules and no test-only fixture.
☐ Run `git diff --check` and `git diff --cached --check`.
☐ Account for every worktree change.
☐ Verify the DFHack process remains responsive and no test-owned screens,
  overlays, input state, or cleanup entries remain.
☐ Record all focused, full, syntax, package, process, diff, and cleanup
  evidence below.

### Phase 3 exit gate

☐ Focused and full unit, live, and syntax checks pass.
☐ Package and diff checks pass.
☐ Functional behavior and cleanup are independently confirmed.
☐ The older coverage checklist links to the new assertions and contains no
  obsolete rejection requirements.

## Evidence ledger

### Phase 1 evidence

- Initial state on 2026-07-26: repository
  `D:\CODE\DFHack\DwarfSpec`, branch `cli-error-formatting`, remote
  `https://github.com/dsisco11/DwarfSpec.git`, with an empty
  `git status --short`.
- `interaction_target.lua` now exposes `input_screen()` on distinct owned and
  borrowed target classes. Owned targets retain their active host-chain
  resolver; borrowed targets query the current viewscreen per input while
  `assert_current()` and `native_screen()` retain the borrowed base DF screen
  for subject and non-input operations.
- `native_attachment.lua` now acquires and validates only the base native DF
  viewscreen and its widgets. Current input-screen access remains exclusively
  in the borrowed interaction target.
- `ds.lua` has one simulated-input boundary. All keyboard, text, click, button,
  and wheel routes call it, and the only production `simulateInput` call is
  `simulateInput(input_screen, keys)` after a non-nil assertion.
- Existing unit contracts were updated to preserve native subject access
  across top-screen changes while retaining widget removal, replacement,
  invalid-root, partial-cleanup, unmount, pointer, and button-state coverage.
